26: /**
27:  * UH steer / resume — message a running worker across a stop and a restart.
28:  *
29:  * A worker can be stopped but, until now, could not be nudged: its only mid-run
30:  * control was `uh kill`. Command Code (and oh-my-pi and Claude Code) accept
31:  * `--resume <session-id>`, and the adapters already implement `resume_from_run`
32:  * with recovery notes. Steering is therefore: stop the run, then resume its
33:  * native session with the operator's message injected as the first instruction
34:  * of the resumed turn.
35:  *
36:  * A live run is steered by the controller that owns it: `uh steer` validates
37:  * everything first, writes a steer request next to the run's
38:  * `runtime-control.json`, and signals the attempt to stop. The controller's
39:  * recovery loop (`runWithRuntimeRecovery`) consumes that request, labels the
40:  * attempt `steered`, and resumes the same native session with the message — so
41:  * a team worker keeps running inside its team controller and is integrated
42:  * normally. Only when no live controller owns the run does steer fall back to
43:  * cancelling and resuming the session itself.
44:  *
45:  * `uh steer` still costs a stop and a restart of the native session — it is not
46:  * a live channel — but the transcript and the prior work are preserved through
47:  * the native resume.
48:  *
49:  * This module is deliberately free of CLI and adapter wiring: it resolves a run
50:  * through the same discovery `uh ps` uses, refuses the cases it cannot honor,
51:  * and delegates the actual re-execution to an injected runner.
52:  */
53: 
54: /** Runtimes with a native session resume path. */
55: export const RESUMABLE_RUNTIMES = ["oh-my-pi", "command-code", "claude-code"] as const;
56: 
57: export function runtimeSupportsResume(runtime: string): boolean {
58:   return (RESUMABLE_RUNTIMES as readonly string[]).includes(runtime);
59: }
